+Process checks per the skill's "Run and Reflect":
+- **Liveness**: temporarily inverted the `hegel_message_roundtrip` assertion; hegel
+  reported a fully shrunk counterexample (the empty `Message`); reverted.
+- **Exploratory 10x run**: all 15 tests were run once at `test_cases = 1000`
+  (default 100), then reverted. No additional failures surfaced.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+**`Name`/`Label` presentation-format escaping is not reversible by the crate's own
+parsers.**
+
+- **Minimal counterexample** (shrunk by hegel): a `Name` with the single label
+  `[0x00]`. `name.to_ascii()` yields `"\\000"`, and
+  `Name::from_ascii("\\000")` fails with `Malformed label`. The same holds for
+  `Display`/`to_utf8` output and `Name::parse`/`from_utf8`/`from_str`.
+- **Why it's a library bug**: labels are 8-bit clean on the wire
+  (`Label::from_raw_bytes` accepts any 1–63 bytes, and such names decode from
+  real DNS messages — see the RFC 4034 example names like `\001.z.example` quoted
+  in this very file's `Ord` docs). `Label::write_ascii` deliberately emits
+  RFC 1035 §5.1 escapes (`\.`, `\!`, `\000`, `\200`) so that any such name has a
+  presentation form, and `Name::from_encoded_str` implements the matching
+  unescaping (its doc examples parse `bad\056char`). But after unescaping, the
+  parser re-validates every character with the *unescaped* safety rule, so every
+  escape the writer can produce **except `\.`** is rejected: control bytes and
+  bytes ≥ 0x80 (e.g. `\000`, `\200`), and single-escaped printables (`\!`, `\$`,
+  `*` in non-first position, …). The crate therefore prints names it refuses to
+  read back, breaking `to_ascii`→`from_ascii` (and Display→`FromStr`) for
+  every label byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9_]`, non-leading `-`, leading `*`, and `.`.
+- **Code path at fault**:
+  `crates/proto/src/rr/domain/name.rs::from_encoded_str` resolves `\DDD`/`\X`
+  escapes into the label string, then hands it to
+  `crates/proto/src/rr/domain/label.rs::Label::from_ascii`, whose
+  `is_safe_ascii(c, is_first, for_encoding = false)` check rejects the very
+  characters `Label::write_ascii` escapes (it also fails earlier on
+  `s.is_ascii()` for bytes ≥ 0x80, which the escape state machine turned into
+  chars like U+0080). The escaped-octet information ("this char came from an
+  escape") is discarded before validation.
+- The failing test `hegel_name_to_ascii_from_ascii_roundtrip` is kept in place,
+  failing, per instructions. The companion `..._safe_labels` test documents the
+  subset that does round-trip.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Message round-trip with empty TXT records.** The first version of the
+  `Message` round-trip generated `TXT::new(vec![])`. Hegel shrank the failure to
+  a message whose only record is an empty TXT: it *encodes* fine (zero-length
+  RDATA) but `Message::from_vec` rejects it with `InvalidEmptyRecord`, because
+  `read_records` treats zero-length RDATA as an UPDATE-style `Update0` record and
+  forbids it outside `OpCode::Update`. RFC 1035 defines TXT-DATA as "one or more
+  `<character-string>`s", so an empty TXT is outside the record's wire domain —
+  the generator was too broad, and it now draws 1–4 strings. Worth noting for
+  the maintainers though: `TXT::new(vec![])` is constructible and
+  `Message::to_vec` happily emits bytes that the crate's own decoder rejects —
+  an encode/decode asymmetry, even if not a decoder bug.
+- No other property was weakened or dropped. Constraints that exist in the
+  generators (opcode ≤ 15, rcode ≤ 15 without EDNS, TXT strings ≤ 255 bytes,
+  labels 1–63 bytes, names ≤ 255 octets) encode documented wire-format field
+  widths, not test-convenience bounds, and each is commented as such in the code.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill and the Rust reference worked: every documented API behaved as
+described, composites/`one_of!`/`sampled_from`/bounded generators all compiled
+first try, and shrinking was excellent (both real failures shrank to genuinely
+minimal counterexamples — a single `[0x00]` label; a message with one empty TXT).
+Specific notes:
+
+1. **`no_std` crates: `#[hegel::test]` needs `ToString` in scope.** The macro's
+   generated code calls `.to_string()` on the test name. `hickory-proto` is
+   `no_std + alloc`, and in `src/rr/serial_number.rs` (a module that imports
+   nothing from `alloc`) every `#[hegel::test]` failed with
+   `error[E0599]: no method named 'to_string' found for reference '&'static str'`
+   pointing at the *attribute*, not at any user code. Fix:
+   `use alloc::string::ToString;` in the test module. The reference's Setup
+   section covers edition-2015 quirks but not this; a gotcha entry would have
+   saved a confused debugging round (the error location makes the cause quite
+   non-obvious). Emitting `::alloc::...`/fully-qualified paths from the macro
+   would fix it properly.
+2. **The 10x exploratory run is clunky without a global override.** Gotcha 13
+   correctly says there is no `PROPTEST_CASES` equivalent, but for 15 tests
+   across 4 files "temporarily edit `test_cases`" means a sed across files and a
+   careful revert (my sed even mangled a comment that mentioned the attribute).
+   An env-var override in hegel, or a skill suggestion to script the temporary
+   edit, would make this step less error-prone.
+3. **Skill advice that paid off directly**: "construct valid inputs directly"
+   (names are built by appending labels until the 255-octet limit, so
+   maximal-length names appear without any rejection sampling); "biasing
+   generators toward boundary conjunctions" (drawing both compared names from a
+   small shared label pool made equal/shared-suffix comparisons common in the
+   `cmp` oracle test; explicitly generating the 2^31 distance in the RFC 1982
+   test); the liveness check; reading the project's fuzz targets as evidence
+   (both message properties come straight from them).
+4. **Minor**: the reference documents `draw` requires `T: Debug` — worth adding
+   that `#[hegel::composite]` therefore effectively requires the composite's
+   return type to be `Debug` too, which matters when generating third-party
+   types (all of hickory's types happened to derive it).
+5. Counterexample output (`let name = Name("\000");`) printing draws as `let`
+   statements was clear and immediately actionable.
